<DocPage
  title="Reference · Viv"
  description="Every statement form, operator, type and builtin in Viv, and which backend runs each. The builtin tables are generated by running the compiler."
  tree={vivTree()}
  toc={TOC}>
  <h1>Every form, operator and builtin</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    The exhaustive companion to the <a href="/tour">tour</a>. The builtin tables below are
    <strong>generated by running the compiler</strong>. A script compiles a probe program for
    each entry through all three backends and records what each one did. As of
    {builtins.probedAt} there are {builtins.total} builtins; {allEngines} work in all three (the
    interpreter, the C backend and the wasm backend){builtins.total > allEngines && <> and {builtins.total - allEngines} in some of them</>}.
  </p>

  <h2 id="lexical">Lexical structure</h2>
  <dl class="deflist">
    <dt>Numbers</dt>
    <dd>
      A number is a 64-bit float. <code>10</code> lexes as <code>10.0</code>, scientific
      notation works (<code>1e-10</code>), and a 64-bit integer is an <code>Int</code>, declared
      with an annotation (<code>n: Int</code>). <code>%</code> and
      <code>//</code> follow Python's rules for floats (see <a href="#operators">operators</a>);
      <code>mod(a, b)</code> is C's fmod, whose result takes the sign of <code>a</code>.
    </dd>
    <dt>No implicit multiplication</dt>
    <dd>
      A name cannot follow a number directly: <code>2x</code> is an error that says to write
      <code>2 * x</code>. The <code>i</code> of an imaginary literal (<code>2.5i</code>) and the
      <code>e</code> of an exponent (<code>1e-10</code>) are part of the number.
    </dd>
    <dt>Names</dt>
    <dd>
      A name starts with a letter or <code>_</code> and continues with letters, digits and
      <code>_</code>. Letters are Unicode letters, so <code>Δx</code> and <code>σ</code> are
      names. Names that start with <code>viv__</code> are used by the parser for the hidden
      variables of <code>a[i] += x</code> and the longer <code>for</code> forms.
    </dd>
    <dt>The dot rule</dt>
    <dd>
      A <code>.</code> is part of a number only with digits on both sides. So <code>1.5</code> is
      a float, <code>x.y</code> is field access, and <code>1.</code> is neither.
    </dd>
    <dt>Statements end at the newline</dt>
    <dd>
      No semicolons. Inside parentheses or brackets the lexer suppresses newlines, so long
      expressions wrap freely.
    </dd>
    <dt>Comments</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>#</code> to end of line, dropped by the lexer. The annotation markers —
      <code>#|</code> what, <code>#||</code> how, <code>#|||</code> why, <code>#~</code> tags,
      <code>#:</code> variable glossary, <code>#[</code>/<code>#]</code> regions — are comments
      the compiler ignores and <code>viv doc</code> extracts. Above a <code>def</code> they form
      its structured doc.
    </dd>
    <dt>Strings</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>"text"</code>, with the escapes <code>\n</code> <code>\t</code> <code>\r</code>
      <code>\0</code> <code>\\</code> <code>\"</code> <code>\'</code> and <code>\u&#123;1F600&#125;</code>
      (any other backslash is an error), and f-strings, <code>f"x = &#123;x:.3f&#125;"</code>.
      A string is a value: see <a href="#strings">Strings</a>.
    </dd>
    <dt>Docstrings</dt>
    <dd>A bare string as the first statement of a <code>def</code> documents it and compiles to nothing.</dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="statements">Statements</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>x = expr</code></td><td>Assignment. At top level it creates a global; inside a <code>def</code>, a local hoisted to function scope.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>x += e</code> <code>-=</code> <code>*=</code> <code>/=</code></td><td>Compound assignment; desugars to the plain form, <code>x = x + e</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[i, j] += e</code> <code>-=</code> <code>*=</code> <code>/=</code></td><td>Compound assignment on an array element: <code>a[i, j] = a[i, j] + e</code>, with each index evaluated once. An index that is not a number or a variable is computed into a hidden variable first, so <code>c[floor(10 * rand())] += 1</code> draws one random number, not two.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[i, j] = expr</code></td><td>Indexed store, up to rank 4. The base may be any array-valued expression, including a record field.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[lo:hi] = v</code> <code>A[:, j] = v</code></td><td>A write into a <a href="#slices">slice</a>: <code>v</code> is a number, which every selected element takes, or an array of exactly the slice's shape. The value is copied before the write, so <code>s[1:5] = s[0:4]</code> shifts. <code>a[lo:hi] += v</code> and the other compound forms read the slice, combine and write it back, with each bound evaluated once.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>return expr</code></td><td>A <code>def</code> that ends without a <code>return</code> gives nothing: it is called as a statement, and the compiler refuses a use of its value (<code>show f(x)</code>, <code>f(x) + 1.0</code>).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>return a, b</code></td><td>Several results. Every <code>return</code> of the def gives the same number of values, each with one type (a number, a boolean, a complex number, an array, a record); <code>return g(x)</code> passes on the values of a def <code>g</code> that returns as many. The caller unpacks them, <code>a, b = f(x)</code>, on a line of its own, or calls <code>f(x)</code> alone and drops them. <code>f(x) + 1</code>, <code>show f(x)</code> and <code>f</code> as a function value are errors that name <code>f</code>. The def returns a record the compiler declares for it (<a href="#types">Records</a>), so every engine runs it.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a, b = e</code></td><td>Unpacking. <code>e</code> is a call of a def that returns as many values, a list of values (<code>a, b = b, a</code> swaps: every value on the right is computed first, then the targets are assigned left to right), or an array with exactly one element per name, such as <code>B, C, H, W = shape(X)</code>; an array of another length stops the run, located at the line. A target is a name, an element <code>v[i]</code> or a slice <code>v[lo:hi]</code>, and its indices are computed after the right side, as in Python: <code>v[i], v[j] = v[j], v[i]</code> swaps two elements. <code>loss, g = value_and_grad(...)</code> is this form too.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>if c &#123; &#125; else &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>With <code>else if</code> chains.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>while c &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>With <code>break</code> and <code>continue</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>for i to n &#123; &#125;</code></td><td><code>i</code> runs 0 … n−1.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>for i from a to b &#123; &#125;</code></td><td><code>i</code> runs a, a+1, … while it is below b. As in <code>for i to n</code>, <code>to</code> names the end and the end is left out.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>for i from a to b by s &#123; &#125;</code></td><td><code>i</code> runs a, a+s, a+2s, … and stops before reaching b: below b for a positive s, above b for a negative one (<code>for i from 10 to 0 by -3</code> is 10, 7, 4, 1). The loop makes ceil((b − a) / s) passes, none when that is 0 or less, and pass k has <code>i = a + k·s</code>. a, b and s are evaluated once, in that order. A step of 0 is an error: at compile time when it is a number, at run time otherwise.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>for i to n reversed &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>The same values in the opposite order: <code>for t to T reversed</code> is T−1, …, 0 and <code>for i from 1 to n reversed</code> is n−1, …, 1. <code>reversed</code> goes last, after any <code>from</code> and <code>by</code>. These longer forms are rewritten by the parser into <code>for k to count</code> over a hidden counter, with <code>i</code> assigned at the top of each pass, so every engine runs them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>parallel for i to n &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>Sequential in the interpreter; the C backend emits an OpenMP pragma. Only this form: no <code>from</code>, <code>by</code> or <code>reversed</code>. Each pass may write only the elements of a shared array indexed by <code>i</code> (<code>a[i]</code>, <code>m[i, j]</code>, <code>m[:, i] = col</code>, <code>setslab(W, i, mine)</code>, or a def given <code>i</code> as the index it writes at), and may read a shared array it writes only at those elements; a write two passes could share, a write of a global through a def, a <code>break</code> or a <code>return</code> is refused before the run, in every engine. Arrays a pass makes are its own, and are freed when the pass ends.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>show expr</code></td><td>Prints the value. A variable, or <code>fmt</code> of one, is labeled with its name (<code>x: 2</code>); any other expression prints bare, as <code>print</code> would.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>show a, b, c</code></td><td>Several labeled values on one line, two spaces apart: <code>a: 1.5  b: 0.3  v: [1, 2]</code>. A variable, or <code>fmt</code> of one, is labeled with its name, so <code>show fmt(x, 17)</code> prints <code>x: 0.30000000000000004</code>. Arrays print on one line here. It is <code>print("a: ", a, "  b: ", b, ..., sep="")</code>; see <a href="#output">Output</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(...)</code></td><td>Text and values on one line, a space between them; see <a href="#output">Output</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>for x in e &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>Each element of <code>e</code> in order: a string's characters, a list's strings, a vector's numbers. <code>for i, x in enumerate(e)</code> gives the index too, and <code>enumerate(e, 1)</code> (or <code>start=1</code>) counts from 1. <code>for a, b in zip(u, v)</code> walks several sequences together and stops at the end of the shortest. <code>for row in rows(M)</code> gives the rows of a matrix, <code>M[k, :]</code> (an array's rank is known only when the program runs, so <code>for x in M</code> reads numbers and stops on a matrix). <code>e</code> is evaluated once; the parser writes the loop as <code>for k to len(e)</code> with <code>x = e[k]</code> at the top of each pass.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>stop("text")</code></td><td>Ends the run with <code>error: text</code> and the line it is on, exit status 1, in every engine: the error a failed check should give. The argument is one string, a literal or a value (<code>stop(f"no atom &#123;name&#125;")</code>). Output printed before it stays printed (the wasm engine reports the error alone, as for every run-time error).</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>narrate "text"</code></td><td>A progress line. A non-string argument is commentary and compiles to nothing.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>test "name" &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>Runs the body and reports under the name: one <code>PASS name</code> or <code>FAIL name</code> line, however many asserts are inside. A failure makes the process exit nonzero.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>def f(a, b, c = 1.0) &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>A parameter with a default comes after those without and is passed by name only: <code>f(1, 2, c=0.5)</code>; a default may read the parameters before it (<code>scale = 1.0 / sqrt(rows)</code>) and is computed at each call that leaves it out. The front end fills every call out, so it costs nothing at run time; a call through a function value passes its arguments in order. Optional annotations: <code>def f(a: Num, v: Arr) -&gt; Num</code>. Recursion works to a depth of 2500 nested calls, the same in every engine; a call past that stops the run with <code>recursion deeper than 2500 nested calls</code>, located at the def. A def and a top-level variable cannot share a name, even when the def comes from a <code>#! use</code> module: the compiled program has one namespace for both, so every engine refuses it and names the two lines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>step name "text" &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>A named phase of a def (or of the top level), with optional text. A step is a block, not a scope: its variables are the def's. The engines record entering and leaving it, and the explorer, the story and the intent and debug lenses address it as <code>def/name</code>, so its name is unique in the def. Not inside a <code>parallel for</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>step name: statement</code></td><td>A step of one statement, on that statement's line: <code>step attn_residual: X1 = X + Y</code> is <code>step attn_residual &#123; X1 = X + Y &#125;</code> to every engine and tool, hashes included. The statement starts after the <code>:</code> on the same line and ends where a statement ends, so <code>step bump: for i to n &#123;</code> runs to the loop's closing brace, and an <code>else</code> on the next line continues an <code>if</code>. <code>;</code> is a line break, so <code>step a: x = 1; y = 2</code> holds only <code>x = 1</code>. With text: <code>step name "text": statement</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>### … ###</code></td><td>A fenced block of text: everything between two lines that are exactly <code>###</code> is skipped, so prose needs no <code>#</code> in front of it. A <a href="/posts">post</a> keeps its title and its notes in one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>rung "title" &#123; &#125;</code></td><td>One step of a <a href="/ladders">ladder</a>: defs, tests and statements that build on the rungs before it. A def in a later rung replaces an earlier one of the same name. <code>viv run</code> runs the top rung; <code>viv ladder</code> runs every rung and checks the ladder's rules.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>record P &#123; x: Num, v: Arr &#125;</code></td><td>Record type (a user-defined struct), in every engine. Build one with every field named, <code>p = P &#123; x: 1, v: zeros(3) &#125;</code>; read a field with <code>p.x</code>; assign one with <code>p.x = 2</code>; write into an array field with <code>p.v[i] = e</code>. A record is a value: <code>q = p</code> gives <code>q</code> its own, and <code>q.x = 3</code> leaves <code>p</code> as it was (an array inside is shared until it is written into, as arrays are). A write is into a variable of the def it is in: a def does not change a record it was given, or a global.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>record M &#123; blocks: [B] &#125;</code></td><td>An array of records: <code>[b1, b2]</code>, <code>append(bs, b)</code>, <code>bs[i]</code>, <code>len(bs)</code>, <code>bs[i] = b</code>, and a place through it, <code>m.blocks[l].mlp.up = W</code> or <code>m.blocks[l].mlp.up[:] = W</code>. A value, as each record in it is.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a + b</code>, <code>zeros_like(r)</code>, <code>for_leaves(f, r1, r2)</code></td><td>Over a record of arrays (every field an array, a record of arrays, or an array of them): <code>+ - * /</code> with another of its type or a number, <code>-r</code>, <code>sqrt(r)</code> and the other elementwise functions, act on each array; <code>zeros_like(r)</code> is one of the same shape; <code>sum(r)</code> adds its arrays' sums; <code>for_leaves(f, r1, ..., x)</code> calls <code>f</code> on each array of the records at once, with <code>x</code> after them, and <code>f</code> may write them in place. <code>loss, g = value_and_grad(f, model, ...)</code> differentiates a record of arrays and gives <code>g</code> of its type. A number field is refused, with its name: keep sizes in a record of their own.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fn(x) -&gt; expr</code></td><td>A lambda, and a value: assign it, pass it, call it through the parameter.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2 id="output">Output with <code>print</code> and <code>fmt</code></h2>
  <p>
    <code>print</code> writes its arguments on one line with a space between them, as Python's
    does, then a newline. <code>sep=", "</code> puts other text between them and
    <code>sep=""</code> none; <code>sep</code> is a string literal or a variable. There is no
    <code>end=</code>: every <code>print</code> ends its line. An argument is a string (written as
    is), <code>fmt(x, ...)</code> (a value in a format), or any other value (as <code>show</code>
    writes it: six significant digits, the way C's <code>%g</code> does). Every value is computed
    before anything is written. The interpreter, C and wasm print the same bytes, checked over
    10<sup>6</sup> doubles (<code>tools/print-fmt-sweep/</code>).
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Writes</th><th>Python</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>print("loss", loss)</code></td><td><code>loss 0.693147</code></td><td><code>print(f"loss &#123;loss:g&#125;")</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print("loss", fmt(loss))</code></td><td><code>loss 0.6931471805599453</code>: the shortest digits that read back as the same double</td><td><code>print("loss", loss)</code> (<code>repr</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(a, b, c, sep=", ")</code></td><td><code>1, 2, 3</code></td><td>the same</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print("[", x, "]", sep="")</code></td><td><code>[0.5]</code></td><td>the same</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fmt(x, 17)</code></td><td>17 significant digits, C's <code>%.17g</code>; any count 1 to 17</td><td><code>&#123;x:.17g&#125;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fmt(x, ".6g")</code>, <code>".3e"</code>, <code>".2f"</code></td><td>C's <code>%.6g</code>, <code>%.3e</code>, <code>%.2f</code></td><td>the same spec</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fmt(x, "10.4f")</code></td><td>at least 10 characters, padded on the left</td><td>the same spec</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>fmt(n, "d")</code>, <code>"5d"</code></td><td>a whole number, no decimal point (<code>%.0f</code>)</td><td><code>&#123;n&#125;</code>, <code>&#123;n:5d&#125;</code> for an int</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(v)</code>, <code>fmt(v, ".2f")</code></td><td>an array on one line, <code>[1, 2.5]</code>, <code>[[1, 2], [3, 4]]</code>, each element in the format</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(flag, z)</code></td><td><code>true 1+2i</code>: booleans and complex numbers, bare only</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(x)</code></td><td>one number: the one-argument <code>print</code>, unchanged</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print()</code></td><td>an empty line</td><td><code>print()</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A format is <code>[width][.precision]</code> then <code>e</code>, <code>f</code>, <code>g</code>
    or <code>d</code>, or empty for <code>repr</code>; precision and width are at most 40 and 100.
    The rest of Python's spec (fill, alignment, <code>+</code>, <code>,</code>, <code>%</code>, a
    precision with no type) is refused with its location and what to write instead. The digit count
    and the format are written in the source, not computed. <code>nan</code> prints as
    <code>nan</code> whatever its sign, and <code>fmt</code> keeps the sign of a negative zero
    (<code>-0.0</code>, <code>-0</code>) where a bare value writes <code>0</code>. A program's own
    <code>def fmt</code> or <code>def print</code> replaces the builtin, and no separator is added to its calls.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Until 2026-09-30 <code>print</code> put nothing between its arguments, and programs wrote
    <code>print(a, " ", b)</code>. <code>tools/migrate_print_sep.py</code> rewrote every program
    in the repository to the new form, checked call by call to reach the engines unchanged, so
    each prints the bytes it printed before.
  </p>

  <h2 id="operators">Operator precedence</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Operators</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="num">1</td><td><code>x ^ y</code></td><td>power, the same as <code>pow(x, y)</code> (so an array base works elementwise). Right-associative, and tighter than a minus on its left: <code>-x ^ 2</code> is <code>-(x ^ 2)</code>, <code>2 ^ 3 ^ 2</code> is 512, <code>2 ^ -1</code> is 0.5</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">2</td><td><code>-x</code> <code>not x</code></td><td>unary</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">3</td><td><code>*</code> <code>/</code> <code>@</code> <code>%</code> <code>//</code></td><td><code>*</code> on two arrays is elementwise; <code>@</code> is always the matrix product (NumPy's <code>matmul</code>: a stack of matrices multiplies matrix by matrix, under <a href="#arrays">Axes</a>). <code>a % b</code> is the floored remainder and <code>a // b</code> the floor of the quotient, Python's rules for floats: the remainder takes the sign of <code>b</code>, so <code>-7 % 3</code> is 2 and <code>(0 - 1) // 2</code> is -1, and <code>a == (a // b) * b + a % b</code>. They take numbers; a zero divisor stops the program with <code>division by zero in <code>a % b</code></code></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">4</td><td><code>+</code> <code>-</code></td><td>elementwise on arrays; all four arithmetic ops broadcast between an array and a scalar, either side, and between two (not complex) arrays by NumPy's rule (<a href="#arrays">below</a>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">5</td><td><code>&lt;</code> <code>&lt;=</code> <code>&gt;</code> <code>&gt;=</code> <code>==</code> <code>!=</code> <code>in</code> <code>not in</code></td><td><code>x in a</code>: a number in a real array of any rank (some element <code>==</code> x, so a NaN is in nothing, as in NumPy), a key in a map, a substring in a string, a string in a list of strings. On numbers, the comparisons give a Bool. With a real array on either side they compare elementwise and give an array of 1.0 where the comparison holds and 0.0 where it does not, so <code>dY * (Y &gt; 0.0)</code> is ReLU's adjoint on any rank. <code>all</code>, <code>any</code> and <code>where</code> read such a mask; <code>if a == b</code> on arrays is refused, with a note that says to write <code>all(a == b)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">6</td><td><code>and</code> <code>or</code></td><td>short-circuit in every engine: the right side runs only when the left does not decide, so <code>j &gt; 0 and a[j - 1] &gt; v</code> never reads <code>a[-1]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td class="num">7</td><td><code>a if c else b</code></td><td>a when c is true, else b; only the one chosen is computed. Loosest of all, as in Python: <code>a if c else b if d else e</code> is <code>a if c else (b if d else e)</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p class="note">
    Measured: <code>2.0 + 3.0 * 4.0</code> is 14, <code>-7 % 3</code> is 2 and <code>7 // -2</code>
    is -4. Parentheses override everything.
  </p>

  <h2 id="comprehensions">Comprehensions, <code>if</code> expressions and keys</h2>
  <p>
    A comprehension builds an array, or a list of strings, from loops, as Python's does. Its
    <code>for</code> takes any head a <code>for</code> statement takes, and its <code>if</code>
    keeps the elements it holds for:
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`xs = [3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9]
evens = [x for x in xs if x % 2 == 0]           # [4]
squares = [i * i for i to 5]                    # [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]
table = [[i * j for j from 1 to 4] for i from 1 to 4]
words = [upper(w) for w in split("a bb ccc")]   # ['A', 'BB', 'CCC']
print(" ".join(f"{x:.2f}" for x in xs[:3]))     # 3.00 1.00 4.00
print(all(x > 0 for x in xs), max(words, key=len))
sign = "neg" if xs[-1] < 0 else "pos"
print(sorted(words, key=len, reverse=true))`} />
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>[e for x in xs]</code>, <code>[e for i to n if c]</code></td><td>a vector of numbers, a matrix when every <code>e</code> is a vector of one length (a row each), or a list of strings. More <code>for</code>s nest, the first outermost; <code>xs</code> may be an array, a string, a list of strings, <code>enumerate(...)</code>, <code>zip(...)</code> or <code>rows(M)</code>. An empty result is <code>[]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;k: v for x in xs&#125;</code></td><td>a <a href="#maps">map</a>, Python's dict comprehension: <code>stoi = &#123;c: i for i, c in enumerate(vocab)&#125;</code>. A key seen again keeps its first place and takes the later value. The same heads and filters as a list's. A brace in an expression is only this: a map literal is <code>map()</code> and stores, and a set (<code>&#123;x, y&#125;</code>) is refused with a pointer to <code>unique</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>f(e for x in xs)</code></td><td>a generator as the only argument of a call: <code>f([e for x in xs])</code>, so <code>sum</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>" ".join</code> take one</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>all(c for x in xs)</code>, <code>any(...)</code></td><td>a Bool, and the loop stops at the first element that decides it, as Python's does</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a if c else b</code></td><td>a when c holds, else b; only the one chosen is computed</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>max(xs, key=f)</code>, <code>min(xs, key=f)</code></td><td>the first element whose key is largest (smallest), as Python's; a NaN key is never chosen after the first element; an empty <code>xs</code> stops with <code>max() arg is an empty sequence</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sorted(xs, key=f, reverse=r)</code>, <code>argsort(xs, key=f, reverse=r)</code></td><td>the elements (or their positions) in the order of their keys, numbers or strings: stable, so equal keys keep their order, with <code>reverse=true</code> too, as Python's; a NaN key goes after every number</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A key is a def's name, a builtin's (<code>len</code>, <code>abs</code>), a variable holding a
    function, or a lambda <code>fn(w) -&gt; ...</code>, which may read the program's variables. A
    comprehension's variables are its own: <code>x</code> outside it is neither read nor changed,
    as in Python 3. The parser writes each of these forms as the loop it stands for, computed
    just before the statement that uses it, so every engine runs code it already runs; what a
    statement computes before it (a call of a def, a <code>print</code>) still runs first, and
    in a <code>while</code> condition it is computed again each time. For that reason one cannot
    be the body of a <code>fn(...) -&gt; ...</code> (except a key's): write a def.
  </p>

  <h2 id="slices">Slices</h2>
  <p>
    A range inside the brackets selects a block of an array, and the result is a <strong>copy</strong>,
    never a view: <code>b = a[1:3]</code> is a new array, and writing <code>b</code> leaves
    <code>a</code> alone. (Assignment and parameter passing alias; slices do not.)
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Selects</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>a[lo:hi]</code></td><td>a[lo], a[lo+1], …, a[hi−1]: hi − lo elements, none when lo = hi</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[:hi]</code> <code>a[lo:]</code> <code>a[:]</code></td><td>a missing lo is 0 and a missing hi is the length of the axis</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[lo:hi:s]</code> <code>a[::s]</code></td><td>every s-th element from lo, stopping before hi; s is 1 or more</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>A[i, :]</code> <code>A[:, j]</code></td><td>row i and column j, as vectors: a plain index drops its axis</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>A[r0:r1, c0:c1]</code></td><td>a block, still a matrix; any rank up to 4 mixes ranges and indices the same way</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>X[lo:hi]</code> on a matrix</td><td>rows lo … hi−1: axes left out at the end are taken whole, as in NumPy</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Bounds are read as an index is (a fraction is cut toward zero), and a negative index or bound
    counts from the end, as in Python: <code>a[-1]</code> is the last element,
    <code>a[-3:]</code> the last three, <code>a[:-1]</code> all but the last, <code>A[-1, :]</code>
    the last row. Nothing is clamped: after that a range needs <code>0 ≤ lo ≤ hi ≤ n</code>, and
    anything else stops the run with its line in every engine, for example
    <code>slice end 4 is past the end of dimension 0 (size 3)</code>, where NumPy would clamp. A
    step of 0 or less is an error, so <code>a[::-1]</code> is not a reversal of an array (it is of
    a string).
  </p>
  <p>
    Four builtins go with slices. <code>copy(a)</code> is a new array with a's shape and elements.
    <code>concat(a, b, ...)</code> joins vectors end to end. <code>vcat(A, B, ...)</code> stacks
    rows and <code>hcat(A, B, ...)</code> puts matrices side by side; to <code>vcat</code> a vector
    is one row and to <code>hcat</code> it is one column, so <code>hcat(u, v)</code> is
    <code>transpose(vcat(u, v))</code> and <code>hcat(t)</code> turns a vector into an n × 1
    matrix. All of them work on complex arrays, and <code>grad</code> differentiates through
    slices, slice writes and all four.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    The parser writes a slice as a call, <code>a[lo:hi, j]</code> as
    <code>viv__slice_get(a, [1, lo, hi, 1, 0, j, 0, 1])</code>, four numbers per index, so the
    interpreter, C and wasm share one set of rules (<code>lang/viv/src/interp/slices.rs</code>).
    Views that share storage would need their own rules for the arena and for aliasing, and are
    a separate design.
  </p>

  <h2 id="growable">Growable arrays</h2>
  <p>
    <code>[]</code> is an empty vector, and <code>a = append(a, x)</code> is <code>a</code> with
    <code>x</code> on the end, so an output whose length is found while scanning needs neither a
    count pass nor a buffer sized by hand:
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`kept = []
for i to len(s) {
    if s[i] > 0.0 {
        kept = append(kept, s[i])
    }
}`} />
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>a = append(a, x)</code>, x a number</td><td>vector a with x after its last element</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a = append(a, r)</code>, r a vector</td><td>matrix a with r as a new last row; an empty <code>[]</code> takes a first row and becomes 1 × len(r)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a = extend(a, b)</code></td><td>vector a followed by vector b (b may be a)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    An append gives back a new array, and it is written rebinding the name it grows, with the
    same name on both sides; <code>b = append(a, x)</code>, an append inside an expression and
    one in a <code>parallel for</code> are refused before the program runs. An alias taken
    before the append keeps the array as it was: after <code>b = a</code> and
    <code>a = append(a, 9.0)</code>, <code>b</code> has the old length, and a write into
    <code>a</code> does not reach it. Each engine grows the storage in place, doubling its room,
    when no other name, value or argument holds the array, and copies otherwise, so a loop of n appends costs O(n)
    and nothing a program prints depends on the room. <code>grad</code> differentiates through
    both. There is no <code>pop</code>: <code>a = a[:-1]</code> drops the last element (a
    copy). Adding a number to a matrix, a vector to a non-empty vector, or a row of the wrong
    length stops the run with its line, for example <code>`append`: the row has 2 numbers, and
    the matrix has 3 columns</code>.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Why rebinding, not <code>push(a, x)</code>: an array handle is copied by value in C, so an
    in-place push would leave an alias with the old length, or pointing at freed memory after
    a reallocation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="arrays">Axes, broadcasting, and agreement with NumPy</h2>
  <p>
    The array builtins take NumPy's names and give NumPy's results: reshapes and transposes,
    <code>eye</code>, <code>diag</code>, <code>arange</code>, reductions along an axis, gathers
    and masks, <code>cumsum</code>, <code>argsort</code>, <code>einsum</code>, and solvers that
    return what they compute. Their tables are under <a href="#builtins">Builtins</a>; the rules
    they share are here.
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`X = reshape(arange(12), 3, 4)
Xc = X - mean(X, 0)                 # the column means, broadcast down the rows
C = transpose(Xc) @ Xc / 3.0
vals, vecs = eig_sym(C)
w = lin_solve(C + eye(4), ones(4))
top = argsort(vals)
picked = take(X, [2.0, 0.0], 0)     # X[[2, 0], :]
big = extract(X > 5.0, X)           # X[X > 5]`} />
  <dl class="deflist">
    <dt>Broadcasting</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>+ - * /</code>, the comparisons and two-array <code>max</code> and <code>min</code>
      between two arrays of different shapes (not complex ones) line up their last axes; each pair of lengths
      is equal, or one of them is 1 and repeats. So a row vector against a matrix repeats down its
      rows, <code>reshape(v, n, 1)</code> is a column that repeats across, and shapes that do not
      fit stop with the words they always did: <code>array shape mismatch: [2, 3] vs [3, 2]</code>.
      Complex arrays do not broadcast. <code>grad</code> sums a broadcast operand's gradient back
      to its shape.
    </dd>
    <dt>Matrix products</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>A @ B</code> is NumPy's <code>matmul</code>. Two matrices give their product; a
      vector on the left is a row and on the right a column, and the axis it adds is dropped. An
      array of rank 3 or 4 is a stack of matrices in its last two axes, and the axes before them
      broadcast, so <code>X @ W</code> with <code>X</code> of shape (4, 8, 16) and <code>W</code>
      (16, 8) multiplies each of the four matrices by <code>W</code>. Two vectors are refused
      (<code>dot(u, v)</code> is their product). Each entry adds its terms in order of the inner
      index from 0.0, skipping a term whose left factor is zero, with a separate multiply and add
      (no fused multiply-add); the loop is blocked for the cache without changing that order, so
      every engine gives the same bits (<code>lang/viv/src/interp/matmul.rs</code>). NumPy calls
      BLAS, which sums in its own order and agrees to rounding.
    </dd>
    <dt>Axes</dt>
    <dd>
      An axis is 0 to rank − 1, or −rank to −1 counted from the end, as in NumPy and as an
      index is. A reduction along an axis (<code>axis=</code>) drops it; with
      <code>keepdims=true</code> it keeps it with length 1, as NumPy's does, which is what lets
      <code>A - mean(A, axis=1, keepdims=true)</code> subtract each row's mean. A builtin's
      options are always given by name, with NumPy's names (<code>axis=</code>,
      <code>keepdims=</code>, <code>k=</code> for <code>triu</code>/<code>tril</code>/<code>diag</code>,
      <code>offset=</code> for <code>trace</code>, <code>endpoint=</code>, <code>axes=</code>);
      written by position they are refused. A reduction along the only axis of a vector would give a number, and is
      refused with the whole-array form to write instead (<code>mean(v)</code>).
    </dd>
    <dt>Orders of summation</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>sum</code>, <code>mean</code>, <code>var</code> and <code>std</code>, along an axis
      or of a whole array, and
      <code>trace</code> add in NumPy's order: from 0.0, a contiguous run in blocks of 8192 entries,
      each block by NumPy's pairwise sum; along an axis that is not the last, the slabs in order.
      They agree with NumPy to the bit (<code>tools/py2viv/probe_numpy_order.py</code> states
      the order), so <code>mean(a)</code> is <code>sum(a) / numel(a)</code> to the bit. The order
      binds the exact engines; fast mode may add in another.
      <code>prod</code>, <code>cumsum</code> and <code>cumprod</code> go left to right, as NumPy
      does. <code>dot</code> and <code>norm</code> go left to right; NumPy calls BLAS, which
      agrees to rounding. <code>einsum</code> loops over the output entries in row-major order
      and over the summed indices in their order of first appearance, adding each product to
      0.0; NumPy's einsum has its own loops, so the two agree to rounding.
    </dd>
    <dt>NaN and signed zeros</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>amax</code>, <code>amin</code>, <code>argmax</code> and <code>argmin</code> are
      NumPy's: a NaN wins, and <code>amax</code> puts 0.0 above −0.0. (<code>max(a)</code> of one
      array is older and skips NaN, as C's <code>fmax</code> does.) <code>argsort</code> puts NaN
      last and keeps −0.0 and 0.0 in their order. <code>clip</code> keeps a NaN entry and treats a
      NaN bound as no bound.
    </dd>
    <dt>Masks and indices</dt>
    <dd>
      A mask is an array of 1.0 and 0.0, as a comparison gives, so an array cannot say whether it
      holds truth values or indices. NumPy's <code>a[mask]</code> is <code>extract(mask, a)</code>,
      <code>a[mask] = v</code> is <code>putmask(a, mask, v)</code>, <code>a[idx]</code> along an
      axis is <code>take(a, idx, axis=k)</code>, and <code>np.flatnonzero</code> is
      <code>flatnonzero</code>. The indices <code>take</code> and <code>put</code> read are not
      negative: <code>a[-1]</code> counts from the end, <code>take(a, [-1.0], 0)</code> stops.
    </dd>
    <dt>Solvers that return</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>x = lin_solve(A, b)</code> (a matrix <code>B</code> solves for every column),
      <code>inv(A)</code>, <code>L = cholesky(A)</code>, <code>vals, vecs = eig_sym(A)</code>,
      <code>w, V = eig_herm(A)</code> and <code>U, s, Vt = svd(A)</code> run the same code as the
      forms that fill arrays you pass, which still work, and give the same bits. The compiler
      writes an unpacking of <code>eig_sym</code>, <code>eig_herm</code> or <code>svd</code> as
      that call with arrays it allocates. <code>svd</code> is one-sided Jacobi
      (<code>lib/svd.viv</code>'s algorithm); its vectors can have other signs than LAPACK's.
    </dd>
  </dl>
  <p class="note">
    <code>tools/arrays/numpy_cases.py</code> writes 4,812 cases whose results NumPy computes in an
    order Viv follows, and 89 that agree with NumPy within a tolerance (<code>einsum</code>, the solvers,
    <code>dot</code>); <code>cargo test --test arrays</code> runs them in the interpreter, C and
    wasm, which print NumPy's results byte for byte.
  </p>

  <h2 id="strings">Strings</h2>
  <p>
    A string is an immutable UTF-8 value. Length, indices and slices count code points, as
    Python's do (<code>len("héllo")</code> is 5), negative indices count from the end, and slice
    bounds are clamped (<code>s[:80]</code> of a short line is the line). Every function below is
    Python's, character for character: its whitespace, its Unicode tables (CPython 3.11's), its
    error messages. <code>a.f(b)</code> is <code>f(a, b)</code> for any call, so text code reads as
    it would in Python:
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`line = "O  0.000  0.757  -0.469"
parts = line.split()
z = float(parts[3])
label = f"{parts[0]}: z = {z:.3f}"
words = []
for w in "a b c".split() {
    words = append(words, w.upper())
}
print(join("-", words), label)`} />
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>a + b</code>, <code>s * n</code>, <code>==</code> <code>&lt;</code> …, <code>sub in s</code></td><td>concatenation, repetition, order by code point, substring (for a list of strings, membership)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>len(s)</code>, <code>s[i]</code>, <code>s[a:b:k]</code>, <code>for c in s</code></td><td>code points; one character; a slice (any step but 0); each character</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>str(x)</code>, <code>repr(x)</code>, <code>fmt(x[, spec])</code></td><td>a number as Python's <code>repr</code> (<code>str(3.0)</code> is <code>"3.0"</code>); a string in quotes; a value in a format, anywhere (not only in <code>print</code>). A string's spec is Python's <code>[[fill]align][width][.precision]</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>f"a &#123;x&#125; &#123;y:.2f&#125; &#123;s!r&#125; &#123;x=&#125;"</code></td><td><code>str(x)</code>, <code>fmt(y, ".2f")</code>, <code>repr(s)</code>, <code>"x=" + repr(x)</code> joined; a field may hold string literals</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>float(s)</code>, <code>int(s[, base])</code></td><td>CPython's parsing (whitespace, <code>_</code> between digits, <code>inf</code>, <code>nan</code>, Unicode digits), correctly rounded; <code>int</code> gives the double nearest the integer</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>upper</code> <code>lower</code> <code>strip</code> <code>lstrip</code> <code>rstrip</code> <code>split</code> <code>rsplit</code> <code>splitlines</code> <code>join</code></td><td>Python's, with full Unicode case mapping (<code>upper("ß")</code> is <code>"SS"</code>)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>find</code> <code>rfind</code> <code>index</code> <code>rindex</code> <code>count</code> <code>replace</code> <code>startswith</code> <code>endswith</code> <code>contains</code></td><td>Python's, positions in code points; <code>index</code> stops with <code>substring not found</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>ljust</code> <code>rjust</code> <code>center</code> <code>zfill</code> <code>chr</code> <code>ord</code> <code>isspace</code> <code>isalpha</code> <code>isalnum</code> <code>isdigit</code> <code>isdecimal</code> <code>isnumeric</code> <code>isupper</code> <code>islower</code> <code>isascii</code> <code>isprintable</code></td><td>Python's</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sorted(s)</code>, <code>unique(s)</code>, <code>unique(words)</code></td><td>Python's <code>sorted(s)</code>: a string's characters as a list, in code point order; and <code>sorted(set(s))</code>: each character once, or each string of a list once, in that order. Viv has no set type: a Python set of strings iterates in an order that changes from run to run, and the sorted list is the form a program can print and test. <code>sort</code> is for arrays of numbers, in place, and refuses a string or a list</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>read_text(p)</code>, <code>read_lines(p)</code>, <code>write_text(p, s)</code></td><td>a UTF-8 text file (newlines as Python reads them); in a browser, the files attached to the program</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A list of strings (<code>[Str]</code>) comes from <code>split</code>, a literal
    <code>["a", "b"]</code>, or <code>[]</code> grown by <code>a = append(a, s)</code>; it has
    <code>len</code>, indices, slices, <code>+</code>, <code>in</code>, <code>sorted</code>,
    <code>unique</code>, <code>join</code> and <code>for</code>. Strings and lists are never changed in place:
    <code>s[0] = "x"</code> and <code>words[0] = "x"</code> are refused, so no alias can see a
    change. A string can be a record field (<code>sym: Str</code>), a parameter, a result, one of
    several results, a figure title or a <code>say</code> caption. An index out of range, a text
    <code>float</code> cannot read and the other Python errors stop the run with CPython's words
    and the line.
  </p>

  <h2 id="maps">Maps</h2>
  <p>
    <code>map()</code> is an empty map. Its keys are numbers or strings, and so are its values:
    one kind of key and one kind of value in each map, set by what the program stores.
    <code>m[k] = v</code> stores,
    <code>m[k]</code> reads, and the keys stay in the order they were first stored, so what a
    program prints never depends on how the keys hash:
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`counts = map()
for i to len(xs) {
    counts[xs[i]] = get(counts, xs[i], 0) + 1
}
ks = keys(counts)
for i to len(ks) {
    print(fmt(ks[i], "d"), fmt(counts[ks[i]], "d"))
}
words = map()
for w in split("the cat and the hat") {
    words[w] = get(words, w, 0) + 1
}
print(words)                   # {'the': 2, 'cat': 1, 'and': 1, 'hat': 1}`} />
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Gives</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>m[k] = v</code></td><td>v under k: a key already there changes its value and keeps its place; a new key goes last</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>&#123;k: v for x in xs if c&#125;</code></td><td>a new map, stored into in the loop's order: <code>&#123;c: i for i, c in enumerate(unique(text))&#125;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>m[k]</code>, <code>m[k] += v</code></td><td>the value under k; a key that is not there stops the run</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>has(m, k)</code></td><td><code>true</code> when k is a key of m</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>get(m, k, d)</code></td><td>m[k] when k is there, else d</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>remove(m, k)</code>, <code>remove(m, k, d)</code></td><td>takes k out and gives its value; without d, a key that is not there stops the run</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>keys(m)</code>, <code>values(m)</code></td><td>a new vector of the keys, or the values, in insertion order (a list of strings for strings)</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>len(m)</code>, <code>copy(m)</code>, <code>clear(m)</code></td><td>the number of keys; a new map with the same pairs in the same order; every key taken out</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(m)</code>, <code>show m</code></td><td><code>&#123;3: 2, 1: 2&#125;</code>, each number as <code>show</code> writes it; a string key or value in its repr, as Python prints a dict: <code>&#123;'a': 1&#125;</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>print(fmt(m, "d", ""))</code></td><td>the keys in the first format and the values in the second, here Python's repr of a dict with int keys and float values: <code>&#123;1: 2.5, 3: 4.0&#125;</code></td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    A map is one handle, changed in place: after <code>b = m</code>, a key stored through
    <code>b</code> is in <code>m</code>, and a def that stores into a map parameter stores into
    the caller's map, as a record field holding a map does. <code>copy(m)</code> is a second map.
    Keys compare with <code>==</code>, so <code>0.0</code> and <code>-0.0</code> are one key (the
    first stored is kept, as in Python); a NaN key stops the run (<code>a map key cannot be
    NaN</code>), and a lookup with a NaN finds nothing. A loop over a map is a loop over a
    snapshot of its keys, <code>ks = keys(m)</code>, so the loop may change the map. Every
    operation is O(1) on average; 10<sup>6</sup> stores and 10<sup>6</sup> reads take about
    0.07 s compiled to C, and with string keys about 0.3 s beyond making the strings. A string
    key hashes and compares by its UTF-8 bytes, so <code>"é"</code> and <code>"e\u{301}"</code> are
    two keys. A map owns a copy of each string stored in it, and a read gives a new string. A map
    that stores a number key and a string key (or both kinds of value), an array, a record or a
    map as a value, a map as an array element, a map inside <code>grad</code> or a
    <code>parallel for</code>, and two keys (<code>m[i, j]</code>) are refused before the run.
    The layout is CPython's compact dict.
  </p>

  <h2 id="types">Types</h2>
  <dl class="deflist">
    <dt>Num</dt>
    <dd>
      f64, and the default: a literal is a Num. The other scalars are declared: <code>Int</code>
      (a 64-bit integer, <code>n: Int</code>), <code>Float32</code> (IEEE binary32) and
      <code>Cplx</code> (below). A double holds every integer up to 2<sup>53</sup> exactly, so
      counters, indices and <code>n == 1.0</code> are exact.
    </dd>
    <dt>Bool</dt>
    <dd>
      <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>: what a comparison yields and what <code>if</code> and
      <code>while</code> take. It can be stored, passed and returned (<code>def f(x: Num) -&gt; Bool</code>),
      combined with <code>and</code>, <code>or</code>, <code>not</code>, and compared with
      <code>==</code> and <code>!=</code>. It is <strong>not a number</strong>: <code>true + 1.0</code>
      and <code>if count &#123;</code> are refused, identically, by the interpreter and the compiler.
      <code>show</code> prints it as <code>true</code> or <code>false</code>. Arrays hold numbers,
      so a mask is still an array of 1.0 and 0.0: a comparison with an array on either side makes
      one, and <code>all(m)</code> and <code>any(m)</code> turn one back into a Bool. See
      <a href="/docs/booleans">booleans</a>.
    </dd>
    <dt>Str</dt>
    <dd>
      An immutable UTF-8 string, indexed by code point (<a href="#strings">Strings</a>). A list of
      them is <code>[Str]</code>.
    </dd>
    <dt>Arr</dt>
    <dd>
      Rank 1 to 4, row-major, f64 elements. Arrays pass into functions and return from them, and
      scalars broadcast against them from either side, and two arrays (not complex ones) broadcast as NumPy's
      do (<a href="#arrays">Axes, broadcasting, NumPy</a>).
      <strong>Assignment and parameter passing alias:</strong> <code>b = a</code> shares storage,
      and a function that writes into an array parameter writes into the caller's array. A real
      copy is <code>copy(a)</code>, and a <a href="#slices">slice</a> is a copy too — see <a href="/docs/scope">scope &amp; mutation</a>.
      Indices count from 0, and an index outside its dimension stops the run in the interpreter and in C with
      the same message, <code>index 2 out of bounds for dimension 0 (size 2)</code>, and the
      line it happened on. A negative index counts from the end, as in Python: <code>a[-1]</code>
      is the last element, and <code>a[-3]</code> of two elements stops the run. A fractional
      index is cut toward zero, so <code>a[1.7]</code> is <code>a[1]</code>.
    </dd>
    <dt>Map</dt>
    <dd>
      Numbers under number keys, in the order the keys were first stored, made by
      <code>map()</code>. A handle: assignment, parameters and record fields share one map. A
      parameter may be annotated <code>m: Map</code>. See <a href="#maps">Maps</a>.
    </dd>
    <dt>Cplx</dt>
    <dd>
      A first-class complex scalar: the <code>i</code> suffix on a numeric literal
      (<code>2.0 + 3.0i</code>), full arithmetic with real promotion, and complex overloads of
      <code>abs/exp/log/sqrt/sin/cos/tan</code>. Compiled to C99 <code>double complex</code>.
      Every engine computes these the same way, to the bit, with Viv's own formulas rather than
      C's operators or <code>&lt;complex.h&gt;</code>:
      <ul>
        <li>A real operand is lifted to <code>(x, 0.0)</code> first, so <code>2.0 * z</code> is the
          complex product with its zero terms.</li>
        <li><code>+</code> and <code>-</code> work part by part.</li>
        <li><code>a * b</code> is <code>(ar br − ai bi) + i (ar bi + ai br)</code>, each product
          rounded on its own (no fused multiply-add).</li>
        <li><code>a / b</code> is Smith's algorithm (1962): divide through by the larger part of
          <code>b</code>. If <code>|br| ≥ |bi|</code>, then with <code>r = bi/br</code> and
          <code>d = br + bi r</code> the quotient is <code>((ar + ai r)/d, (ai − ar r)/d)</code>;
          otherwise <code>r = br/bi</code>, <code>d = bi + br r</code>, and the quotient is
          <code>((ar r + ai)/d, (ai r − ar)/d)</code>. It never squares <code>b</code>, so it
          overflows only where the quotient does, not once <code>|b|</code> passes
          1e154 as the textbook <code>a conj(b) / |b|²</code> does. A real divisor divides each
          part once, so <code>(1 + 2i) / 3</code> is exactly <code>1/3 + (2/3)i</code>. Dividing by
          zero gives <code>nan + nan i</code>.</li>
        <li><code>abs(z)</code> is <code>hypot(re, im)</code>. <code>exp</code> is
          <code>e^re (cos im + i sin im)</code>. <code>log</code> is
          <code>ln|z| + i atan2(im, re)</code>. <code>sqrt</code> is <code>√|z|</code> at half the
          argument. <code>sin</code> and <code>cos</code> use the <code>cosh</code>/<code>sinh</code>
          formulas, and <code>tan</code> is <code>sin z / cos z</code> by the division above. All of
          them use Viv's libm.</li>
      </ul>
      A complex array divided by a scalar divides each element the same way.
    </dd>
    <dt>CArr</dt>
    <dd>
      An array of complex numbers, rank 1 to 4: <code>czeros(n)</code>, or a literal with a complex
      element in it (<code>[1.0 + 2.0i, 3.0]</code> — the reals promote). Indexing gives a
      <code>Cplx</code>; storing a real promotes it. It is <strong>packed</strong>: interleaved
      (re, im) doubles, which is exactly how C99 lays out <code>double complex</code>, so both
      engines walk the same memory and <code>fft</code> agrees between them to the byte. A complex
      and a real array are different types and do not mix silently — <code>sin(a)</code> on a
      complex array is an error. Annotate a parameter as <code>CArr</code> when you
      want to say so; otherwise it is inferred from the call site.
    </dd>
    <dt>Records</dt>
    <dd>
      User-defined structs with typed fields, copied by value: the C backend compiles them to C
      structs, the wasm backend to a block of memory, and the interpreter holds the fields. Array
      fields refer to the original array, so bundling a mesh into one value copies no array data.
      A def that takes or returns one annotates it (<code>def f(p: P) -&gt; P</code>). Records have
      no operators and no <code>show</code>: compare and print their fields. The several results of
      <code>return a, b</code> are a record the compiler declares for the def, with one field per
      value and the field types inferred from the returns; the program never names it, because
      the caller unpacks it.
    </dd>
    <dt>Function values</dt>
    <dd>
      A <code>def</code> name or a lambda is a value. The compiled backends implement
      higher-order calls by monomorphization — <code>run_it(sq, x)</code> specializes to
      <code>run_it__sq</code> — with <code>viv_fn</code> function pointers for the cases where a
      function is held in a variable. Arrays of functions work in the interpreter and are a compile
      error in C.
    </dd>
  </dl>

  <h2 id="contracts">A note on contracts</h2>
  <p>
    <code>integrate</code> and <code>find_root</code> are not builtins. They are
    <strong>contracts</strong>: functions named for the mathematical problem rather than the
    method, backed by several methods from different families of algorithms. By default they run
    every applicable method and check that the answers agree, and a disagreement stops the
    program with an error. <code>viv run --release</code> runs only the
    one method the contract selects (Gauss quadrature for <code>integrate</code>, a safeguarded
    secant for <code>find_root</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    The methods are ordinary Viv <code>def</code>s in
    <code>lang/viv/std/contracts.viv</code>, brought in with
    <code>viv --use lang/viv/std/contracts.viv program.viv</code>. The methods a contract chooses
    among are Viv code, not code in the compiler.
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    <strong>Interpreter only, today.</strong> A contract picks its method out of an array of
    functions, and the C backend implements higher-order calls by monomorphization — it
    specializes the callee per call site — which cannot express a callee chosen at runtime.
    So the contract library runs in the interpreter, and the C backend stops with a compile error
    naming the feature instead of producing wrong code.
  </p>

  <h2 id="derivatives">Derivatives with <code>deriv</code>, <code>grad</code> and <code>value_and_grad</code></h2>
  <p>
    <code>deriv(fn(x) -&gt; expr, x0)</code> is forward mode: the derivative of one scalar
    expression at a point. <code>grad</code> and <code>value_and_grad</code> are reverse mode: the
    gradient of a whole <code>def</code>, loops, branches, calls and arrays included, with respect
    to its first argument, in one forward and one backward pass.
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`def loss(theta: Arr, X: Arr, y: Arr) {
    h = tanh(X @ theta)
    return sum((h - y) * (h - y)) / len(y)
}

g = grad(loss, theta, X, y)                  # the shape of theta
l, g = value_and_grad(loss, theta, X, y)     # the loss too`} />
  <p>
    The first argument names a <code>def</code>; the others are passed to it unchanged and are
    constants for the derivative. The differentiated argument is a number or a real array, the
    gradient has its shape, and the def returns a number. <code>value_and_grad</code> gives two
    results, written <code>a, b = value_and_grad(...)</code> on its own line. A program that defines
    its own <code>grad</code> keeps it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The compiler rewrites the program before any engine sees it (<code>lang/viv/src/grad.rs</code>),
    so the interpreter, C and wasm run the same generated code and print the same bytes. It adds
    one def per differentiated def: the def's statements, pushing every value an assignment
    overwrites onto a tape, then the adjoint statements in reverse order. A def called with a
    differentiated argument is inlined and differentiated with the caller; a call with constant
    arguments stays a call. Branches go back the way they came, and loops run backward over the
    recorded count. A def that returns several values (<code>return a, b</code>) or a record is
    inlined wherever it is called in differentiated code, and the record it builds becomes one
    variable per field, so <code>y, dy = helper(w, x)</code> inside a loss is differentiated like
    any other assignment; a record that only comes in (an argument, such as a model's
    configuration) is a constant.
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>Adjoint</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>+ - * / %</code>, a number with an array</td><td>the usual rules; an array's gradient is summed back onto a number it was combined with, and onto an array that was broadcast, down to its shape</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>A @ B</code></td><td><code>dA = g @ transpose(B)</code>, <code>dB = transpose(A) @ g</code> (an outer product for a vector operand); for stacks of matrices the last two axes are swapped, and an operand broadcast along the batch axes gets its gradient summed back to its shape</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>sum mean var std prod amax amin</code>, along an axis or of the whole array</td><td>the gradient spread back along the reduced axis (with or without keepdims): <code>mean</code> divides it by the axis's length, <code>var</code> and <code>std</code> multiply by the deviation from the mean, <code>prod</code> by the product of the other entries (right where an entry is zero); <code>amax</code> and <code>amin</code> share it evenly among the entries equal to the result, as <code>torch.amax</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>reshape</code>, <code>ravel</code>, <code>flatten</code>, <code>transpose(a, axes=p)</code>, <code>swapaxes</code>, <code>take(a, idx, axis=k)</code></td><td>the gradient reshaped or permuted back; <code>take</code> along an axis adds each slice back where it was read</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>dot</code>, <code>norm</code></td><td><code>g b</code> and <code>g a</code>; <code>g a / norm(a)</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[i, j]</code>, <code>take</code>, <code>slab</code>, <code>a[lo:hi]</code></td><td>added back into the entries read; an index taken twice collects twice</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>a[i] = e</code>, <code>setslab</code>, <code>put</code>, <code>a[lo:hi] = e</code></td><td>the overwritten value passes nothing back; <code>e</code> gets the slice's adjoint, summed when <code>e</code> is a number</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>copy</code>, <code>concat</code>, <code>vcat</code>, <code>hcat</code></td><td>unchanged; each argument gets its own piece</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>exp log sqrt sin cos tan tanh sinh cosh asin acos atan erf pow atan2</code></td><td>the derivative, from the recorded values</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>abs</code></td><td><code>g sign(x)</code>, 0 at 0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>max(a, b)</code>, <code>min(a, b)</code></td><td>to the larger (smaller) side; half each on a tie, as <code>torch.maximum</code>. So <code>max(x, 0.0)</code> passes <code>g / 2</code> at exactly 0, where <code>torch.relu</code> passes 0</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>max(a)</code>, <code>min(a)</code>, <code>sum</code>, <code>transpose</code>, <code>where</code></td><td>shared among tied entries (<code>torch.amax</code>); spread over <code>a</code>; transposed; by the mask, summed back to the shape of an argument that was broadcast</td></tr>
        <tr><td>comparisons, <code>floor ceil round //</code>, <code>len numel shape zeros</code></td><td>constant: nothing passes back</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Anything else is a compile error at its line, never a silent zero: a builtin without an adjoint
    (<code>gamma</code>, <code>eig_sym</code>, <code>lin_solve</code>, <code>fft</code>,
    <code>sort</code>, complex numbers, records) given a differentiated value; <code>break</code>
    and <code>continue</code>; <code>return</code> inside a loop; <code>parallel for</code>;
    recursion; a read of a variable before its first assignment on some path; <code>b = a</code> for
    an array that is written later through either name while both are in use (write
    <code>b = a + 0.0</code>); a write into a parameter or global array the def also reads; a call
    with a differentiated argument inside an <code>if</code> or <code>while</code> condition; and
    <code>grad</code> inside a def being differentiated. Clamp a value with two <code>if</code>s to
    get <code>torch.clamp</code>'s gradient, which passes at both ends of the interval.
  </p>
  <p>
    The tape holds every value the forward pass overwrites, with no checkpointing. The six
    models in <code>study/idioms</code> that wrote their backward passes by hand each have a copy
    that uses <code>value_and_grad</code>, and the generated gradients match the hand-written ones
    to 1.1e-15 of the largest entry or better. <code>lib/nn</code>'s RMSNorm, softmax and
    cross-entropy are whole-array expressions (reductions along the last axis with keepdims),
    and <code>lib/gpt</code> differentiates a whole batch of sequences in one
    <code>value_and_grad</code>. Derivatives through <code>eig_sym</code> and
    <code>lin_solve</code>, Hessians and checkpointing are not built yet.
  </p>

  <h2 id="phrases">Phrases</h2>
  <p>
    A phrase is an expression written the way a person says it. It starts with <code>take</code>
    followed by a word or a number, or with one of the verbs {phrases.verbs.map((v, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ', '}<code>{v}</code></>))}
    followed by a word or a number. The parser reads it as the call in the second column below, so
    the interpreter, C and wasm run only calls they already run, and a phrase prints what its call
    prints in all three.
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`c = 2.0
M = [[4.0, 1.0, 2.0], [1.5, 5.0, 3.0], [2.0, 3.5, 6.0]]
area = take the integral of x^2 from 1 to 2            # q_gauss(fn(x) -> x^2, 1, 2)
r = solve x^2 = c starting at 1 using newton           # r_newton(...), f' by forward-mode AD
s = solve x^2 = c between 0 and 2 using bisection      # r_bisect(fn(x) -> x^2 - c, 0, 2)
off = take the off-diagonal row sums of M              # sum(M - diag(diag(M)), 1)
x = solve M @ x = [1.0, 2.0, 3.0]                      # lin_solve(M, [1.0, 2.0, 3.0])`} />
  <p>
    The rules:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      A phrase runs to the end of its line, or to a comma, a closing bracket, <code>{'{'}</code>,
      <code>:</code>, <code>if</code> or <code>else</code> outside brackets. So
      <code>take the norm of u / n</code> is the norm of <code>u / n</code>; write
      <code>(take the norm of u) / n</code> for the other reading.
    </li>
    <li>
      The parser tries every pattern and every way to split the words among its holes, and keeps
      the splits whose holes parse as whole expressions. One reading is the phrase. None is an
      error that names the closest patterns; two or more is an error that lists them. It does not
      choose between readings.
    </li>
    <li>
      In an integral, a root or a derivative, the expression is a function of its one free
      variable: the one name in it that the program does not assign at that point. With none or
      several, the phrase is refused until you name it: <code>... with respect to t</code> or
      <code>... for t</code>. An equation <code>l = r</code> is solved as <code>l - r = 0</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      A part the call reads more than once (the median reads its argument four times) is computed
      once, into a hidden variable.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>take</code>, the verbs and every builtin keep working as names: a phrase needs a word
      or a number right after its first word, which no other expression has.
    </li>
    <li>
      A phrase has the content address of its call, so rewording one (<code>mean</code> for
      <code>average</code>) does not make a def's intent stale. <code>viv phrases file.viv</code>
      prints each phrase of a program with the call it is.
    </li>
    <li>
      The quadratures and root finders are the defs of {phrases.std.map((f, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ' and '}<code>{f}</code></>))}.
      A program that uses one of them gets the def added; one that defines its own def of that
      name and uses the phrase is refused.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A program can declare its own phrase at the top level. A word of the phrase that is a name the
    body reads is a hole; the other words are the ones a use must contain:
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`phrase "the Rayleigh quotient of A at x" -> dot(x, A @ x) / dot(x, x)
q = take the Rayleigh quotient of M at e`} />
  <p>
    The table has {phrases.count} patterns, {phrases.knot} of them ported from Knot. Each example
    below is a test in <code>lang/viv/tests/phrases/phrases.viv</code> that checks the phrase
    against the value NumPy or a closed form gives; {phrases.passed} of {phrases.tests} passed in
    the browser compiler when this page was built, and the same file passes in the interpreter,
    as C and as wasm.
  </p>
  {
    phrases.groups.map((g) => (
      <>
        <h3 id={`p-${g.title.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z]+/g, '-')}`}>{g.title}</h3>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Phrase</th>
                <th>Is the call</th>
                <th>Example</th>
                <th class="c">✓</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {g.rows.map((r) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{r.phrase}</code></td>
                  <td><code>{r.call}</code></td>
                  <td><code>{r.example}</code></td>
                  <td class={`c ${r.passed ? 'y' : 'n'}`}>{tick(r.passed)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="builtins">Builtins</h2>
  <p>
    Columns: <b>I</b> the interpreter, <b>C</b> the C99 backend, <b>W</b> the direct wasm backend.
    Each mark is a probe program that compiled (and, for I, ran) when this page was built.
  </p>
  <p>
    A builtin's name is not reserved. A program that writes its own <code>def band(lo, hi)</code>,
    <code>def where(...)</code> or <code>def sin(a, b, c)</code> calls its def everywhere it
    writes that name, in every engine, with the def's own arguments; the builtin is out of reach
    in that program. So a new builtin never changes what an existing program computes.
  </p>
  {
    builtins.groups.map((g) => (
      <>
        <h3 id={`b-${g.title.toLowerCase().replace(/[^a-z]+/g, '-')}`}>{g.title}</h3>
        {g.note && <p class="note" style="margin-top:0.2rem" set:html={codeSpans(g.note)} />}
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Signature</th>
                <th />
                <th class="c">I</th>
                <th class="c">C</th>
                <th class="c">W</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              {g.items.map((b) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{b.sig}</code></td>
                  <td set:html={codeSpans(b.blurb)} />
                  <td class={`c ${b.interp ? 'y' : 'n'}`}>{tick(b.interp)}</td>
                  <td class={`c ${b.c ? 'y' : 'n'}`}>{tick(b.c)}</td>
                  <td class={`c ${b.wasm ? 'y' : 'n'}`}>{tick(b.wasm)}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="reporting">What a test prints</h2>
  <p>
    A <code>test</code> block is the unit that passes or fails, so it prints exactly one line
    under the name you gave it — not one line per assertion:
  </p>
  <Code lang="viv" code={`test "simpson integrates exp correctly" {
    assert_near(simpson(fn(x) -> exp(x), 0.0, 1.0, 60), exp(1.0) - 1.0, 1.0e-9)
}

$ viv run integrate.viv
PASS simpson integrates exp correctly`} />
  <p>
    Several asserts in one block still give one line. A failure names the test and carries the
    first failing assertion's detail, and a block with no assertions says so instead of printing
    PASS:
  </p>
  <pre class="dump">{`PASS two asserts, one verdict
FAIL a failing one -- assert_near: |1 - 2| = 1 > 1e-09
PASS nothing asserted (no assertions)
assert_near PASS
1 assertion FAILED`}</pre>
  <p>
    That last <code>assert_near PASS</code> is an assertion at top level, outside any
    <code>test</code> — there is no name to report under, so it prints its own line as before.
    The trailing count is the summary, and it is what makes the process exit nonzero, so a build
    script can fail on the exit code. It goes to stderr, after everything the program printed, in
    the interpreter and in a compiled program alike, so stdout holds only the program's own output.
  </p>

  <h2 id="backends">The backend matrix</h2>
  <p>The interpreter and the two compilers share one parser. Which features each one runs:</p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Interpreter</th><th>C backend</th><th>Direct wasm</th></tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Scalars, control flow, <code>def</code>, recursion</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arrays</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Lambdas, <code>simpson</code>, <code>deriv</code></td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>grad</code>, <code>value_and_grad</code> (rewritten before any engine)</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Records</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>return a, b</code>, <code>a, b = e</code> (rewritten before any engine)</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Complex scalars</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">C99 <code>double complex</code></td><td class="y">two f64s, the interpreter's formulas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Arrays of functions (for example, a list of methods to try)</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">a table, <code>call_indirect</code></td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>parallel for</code></td><td>sequential</td><td class="y">OpenMP pragma</td><td>compiles, sequential</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>narrate</code></td><td class="y"><code>narrate:</code> line</td><td class="y"><code>narrate:</code> line</td><td class="y"><code>narrate:</code> line</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Figures (<code>plot</code> … <code>scene</code>)</td><td class="y">drawn</td><td class="y">printed as records</td><td class="y">printed as records</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Call graph (<code>__graph__</code>/<code>__trace__</code>)</td><td class="y">✓</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Runaway-loop guard</td><td class="y">step budget</td><td>—</td><td>—</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Nonzero exit on a failed assert</td><td class="y">✓</td><td class="y">✓ (atexit)</td><td class="y">✓ (the host's)</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every feature goes into both the interpreter and the C backend, and the example suite checks
    that it does. The direct wasm backend reaches wasm without C (a sum-of-squares program is a
    {trace.wasmSize}-byte module) and is checked beside them as a third engine
    (<code>tools/three-engines.sh</code>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="cli">The CLI</h2>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Command</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>viv f.viv -o f.c</code></td><td>Compile to portable C99. Then <code>cc f.c -O2 -lm</code>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv f.viv -o f.wasm</code></td><td>Compile straight to a wasm module, no C step.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv f.viv --emit tokens|ast|ir</code></td><td>Dump an intermediate representation. The <a href="/internals">internals page</a> is these dumps.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv run [--release] f.viv</code></td><td>Interpret. The compiler's checks run first, so <code>viv run</code> refuses what the compile refuses, with the same messages: a call to an unknown function in a branch the run never takes, or a variable that holds a number in one place and an array in another. <code>--release</code> runs only the method each contract selects instead of all of them.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv run --quiet f.viv</code></td><td>Drop the <code>__graph__</code>/<code>__trace__</code>/<code>__steps__</code>/<code>__story__</code> lines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv run --json f.viv</code><br /><code>viv f.viv --json</code></td><td>Write the errors to stderr as one line of JSON instead of text: <code>&#123;"diagnostics":[...]&#125;</code>, each with <code>severity</code>, <code>message</code>, <code>file</code>, <code>line</code>, <code>col</code>, <code>end_line</code>, <code>end_col</code>, <code>label</code>, <code>source</code> (the line) and <code>notes</code>. A run with no errors writes an empty list. For tools and models that read the errors and try again.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv --use lang/viv/std/contracts.viv f.viv</code></td><td>Put another file (a prelude) in front of this one before parsing. A program names the modules it uses itself, with import lines.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>import nn</code> &middot; <code>import hf.scf as scf</code> &middot; <code>from nn import rmsnorm, softmax_rows</code></td><td>Python's forms with Python's bindings: <code>nn.rmsnorm(X)</code>, <code>scf.run(...)</code>, <code>rmsnorm(X)</code>; <code>import a.b</code> binds <code>a</code>; a <code>from</code> list may be parenthesized over several lines. Module <code>a.b</code> is <code>a/b.viv</code> beside the file, then <code>lib/a/b.viv</code>, then <code>a/b.viv</code> under the repository root; two different files that match are an error. A module is spliced in once, its tests left out, and its names are its own: a program sees what its import lines bind and not what its imports import, and a name two modules define is two names. Its phrases come with it. <code>import *</code> and relative imports are refused, and so is <code>#! use</code>, with the import to write. Resolved before anything parses: no run-time cost.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>private def f</code> &middot; <code>private N = 3</code></td><td>Keep a top-level def, record, enum or constant in its module: importing it, or reading it from another module, is a compile error.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>import post "slug" as name</code></td><td>A post used as a module.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>#! if-used basis.sto3g</code> &hellip; <code>#! end-if</code></td><td>Keep the lines between only when the program imports that module, anywhere in its imports. A lookup can serve whichever of several modules a program loads: <code>basis.named</code> finds a basis set by name among the tables the program imports.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>#! if-none-used basis.a basis.b</code> &hellip; <code>#! end-if</code></td><td>Keep the lines between, imports included, when the program's other imports reach none of the modules named: a default the program overrides by importing one. <code>basis.named</code> loads all eight basis tables for a program that imports none.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv expand f.viv</code> &middot; <code>viv imports --write f.viv</code> &middot; <code>viv fix --keywords --write f.viv</code></td><td>The program as the engines read it, imports spliced and names resolved; rewrite a file's <code>#! use</code> lines as imports; name the builtin options a file writes by position.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv doc f.viv [fn]</code></td><td>Structured annotations as JSON, or one function's doc card.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv whatis f.viv name</code></td><td>One name explained: kind, type, where it came from.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv lsfunc f.viv</code></td><td>Every <code>def</code> with its parameters.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv lsvar f.viv [fn]</code></td><td>Every binding, with its glossary line if it has one.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv src f.viv fn</code></td><td>One function's source, annotations stripped.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv deps f.viv</code></td><td>The static call graph.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv canon f.viv</code></td><td>Canonical dependency order. Idempotent.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv hash f.viv</code></td><td>Per-definition content addresses: node hash and body hash.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv features [name]</code><br /><code>viv features --usage [paths]</code></td><td>What Viv can do, by area, each entry with what it does, when to use it, an example and its output; <code>--usage</code> counts each feature's uses in a corpus and lists code that does by hand what a feature does. See <a href="#features">the feature catalog</a>.</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>viv diff a.viv b.viv</code></td><td>Structural diff — edits, renames, unchanged.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    An error at run time reads like one at compile time: the message, the file, line and column,
    the line itself, and a caret under the part that failed. A compiled program prints the same
    lines, byte for byte, because the compiler writes each indexing site's location into the C.
  </p>
  <pre class="dump">{runtimeErrorDump}</pre>
  <p>
    The notes answer habits from Python, <code>**</code> (write <code>x ^ y</code>) and
    <code>for i in range(n)</code> (write <code>for i to n</code>), and one from Julia,
    <code>2x</code> (write <code>2 * x</code>).
  </p>
  <p>
    Every one of these commands also runs in the browser, on this site, at
    <a href="/cli">/cli</a> — the wasm module exports the same library functions
    <code>main.rs</code> wraps.
  </p>

  <h2 id="features">The feature catalog</h2>
  <p>
    <code>viv features</code> lists what Viv has: {features.hand} features written up in
    {features.areas.length} areas, and {features.generated} entries generated from the compiler's
    own tables ({features.generatedAreas.map((a, i) => (<>{i > 0 && ', '}{a.heading.toLowerCase()} {a.count}</>))}).
    Each written-up entry has a minimal example, and <code>cargo test --test features</code>
    runs every example in the interpreter, as C and as wasm and checks the output it records. The
    tables below are that catalog; <code>viv features &lt;name&gt;</code> prints one entry with
    its example and output, and <code>viv features --search &lt;words&gt;</code> finds entries by
    words. Agents writing Viv consult it before writing code, and run
    <code>viv features --usage</code> on what they wrote: it lists loops, sorts, roundings and
    copies written out where a feature does the work.
  </p>
  {
    shownAreas.map((a) => (
      <>
        <h3 id={`features-${a.key}`}>{a.heading}</h3>
        <div class="tablewrap">
          <table>
            <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it does</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              {a.entries.map((e) => (
                <tr>
                  <td><code>{e.id}</code></td>
                  <td>{e.what.split('`').map((p, i) => (i % 2 ? <code>{p}</code> : p))}</td>
                  <td>{e.doc.join('; ')}</td>
                </tr>
              ))}
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </>
    ))
  }

  <h2 id="runtime">The C runtime</h2>
  <p>Compiled programs carry their runtime with them. One struct,</p>
  <Code lang="c" code={`typedef struct { int rank; int dims[8]; int size; double* data; } viv_arr;`} />
  <p>
    plus a set of small <code>viv_*</code> functions — array ops, the eigensolvers, the LU and
    Cholesky factorizations, quadrature, the Park–Miller generator, and the show/plot/narrate
    printers — emitted <strong>only on demand</strong>. A program that never touches an array gets
    none of them; the nine-line example on the <a href="/internals">internals page</a> pulls in
    exactly one. Building it takes only <code>cc program.c -O2 -lm</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Every array read and write in the C is checked: the number of indices against the array's
    rank, then each index against its dimension. A wrong count or an index out of bounds stops a
    compiled program with the interpreter's error and location instead of reading memory the
    array does not own. The checks are on by default. Build with
    <code>-DVIV_RELEASE</code> to drop them along with the other debug-build checks, or with
    <code>-DVIV_NO_BOUNDS</code> to drop only them. What they cost, measured with
    <code>tools/bounds-cost.sh</code> (CPU time, best of 7 runs, <code>cc -O2</code>, Apple M2 Pro):
  </p>
  <div class="tablewrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Program</th><th>Checked</th><th>Unchecked</th><th>Cost</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><code>bench/hf_md_eri_x100.viv</code> (water 6-31G ERIs, built 100 times)</td><td>0.385 s</td><td>0.275 s</td><td>+40%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bench/matmul_loops.viv</code> (400×400, all indexing)</td><td>0.105 s</td><td>0.082 s</td><td>+29%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scf/meta_integrals.viv</code></td><td>0.503 s</td><td>0.396 s</td><td>+27%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scf/meta_scf.viv</code></td><td>0.660 s</td><td>0.368 s</td><td>+79%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scf/lock_water.viv</code></td><td>0.200 s</td><td>0.115 s</td><td>+73%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>scf/coverage.viv</code></td><td>0.260 s</td><td>0.145 s</td><td>+80%</td></tr>
        <tr><td><code>bench/hf_scf_bench.viv</code> (water cc-pVDZ RHF)</td><td>0.042 s</td><td>0.023 s</td><td>+84%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    That is 27% to 84% on these programs, so a build you time for speed should pass
    <code>-DVIV_RELEASE</code>; a build you run to check a program should keep them. The
    WebAssembly modules this site serves (<code>dft/wasm/</code>, <code>engines/wasm/</code>) are
    built with <code>-DVIV_RELEASE</code>; the same programs run with the checks on in
    <code>dft/check.sh</code> and in the interpreter and in C. In those modules an index out of range is not
    caught: a write can land in another array of the same program (it cannot leave the module's
    memory). The interpreter and the module the compiler in your browser makes when you press Run
    (the direct wasm backend) check every index and stop with the same error as the C. <code>-DVIV_RELEASE</code> also trusts the
    <code>disjoint</code> pins of a memory lens instead of
    testing them; a checked build records which pass of a pinned <code>parallel for</code>
    writes each element and stops if two passes write the same one.
  </p>
  <p>
    The location in a run-time error names the program as you gave it when the path is
    relative (<code>lib/hf/scf.viv:12:5</code>) and by its file name when the path is
    absolute (<code>scf.viv:12:5</code>), in the interpreter and in C. The C carries those locations, so a
    program compiled by absolute path gives the same C on every machine and in every checkout.
  </p>

  <p class="note">
    The builtin tables were probed at build time ({builtins.probedAt}).
  </p>

